Problem
Autonomous reconfigurable control systems switch controllers in response to detected faults. Each candidate controller may be individually verified as stable in isolation, but the act of switching between controllers is a hybrid/switched system event, and arbitrary switching between stable subsystems can still produce an unstable overall trajectory (a classical and well-documented pitfall in switched systems theory). No existing applied FTC framework formally bounds this risk.

Research Questions


RQ1 (Theoretical): Under what switching-rate conditions can we guarantee
     global exponential stability of a system whose controller is
     reconfigured online in response to detected faults?

RQ2 (Algorithmic): Can a Bayesian probabilistic diagnosis engine
     outperform rule-based threshold diagnosis in classification
     accuracy and false-alarm rate, under identical residual data?

RQ3 (Estimation): Does EKF-based residual generation improve fault
     detectability over an open-loop digital twin, particularly for
     nonlinear plants?

RQ4 (Empirical): Does PHYSIOPT-X show statistically significant
     improvement (via paired hypothesis testing across Monte Carlo
     trials) over PID, LQR, Adaptive-PID and MPC baselines, across
     RMSE, recovery time, and control effort?

RQ5 (Generality): Does the architecture generalize from a linear
     plant (DC motor) to a nonlinear plant (robotic arm) without
     structural redesign of the framework?


